-- allium: 3
-- 18-equity-option-pricing.allium

-- Scope: Valuation of single-underlying equity call options, European and
--   American, on a stock paying discrete cash dividends and a dividend yield
--   (D-022, D-046).
-- Includes: what a valuation needs, what it must return, how the volatility
--   and dividends are read from their market objects, how exercise styles
--   relate, life-cycle events, how the planner expands an option request.
-- Excludes:
--   - The trade payload fields: generated from the Julia types (D-018).
--   - Discount curves, the equity volatility surface and the dividend
--     schedule themselves (08).
--   - Sensitivities (vega, curvature) and FRTB aggregation (11): bumped graph
--     nodes (D-032).
--   - Puts, barriers, baskets, quantos, index options, local volatility
--     (see PlainVanillaOnly).
-- Dependencies: 01, 02, 03, 08.
--
-- Planning (D-020), one pass, for one option position version:
--   calendars named by the trade (03) -> discount curve of the option
--   currency (08) -> dividend schedule of the underlying (08) -> equity
--   volatility surface of the underlying (08) -> valuation node (this spec),
--   with the spot price series of the underlying as input.

use "./01-bitemporal-data.allium" as data
use "./02-calculation-graph.allium" as graph

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract EquityOptionValuation {
    value: (position: data/InputVersion, as_of: Timestamp, discount_curve: String, vol_surface: String, dividends: String, spot: data/InputVersion, calendars: Set<data/InputVersion>, reporting_currencies: Set<String>, fx: Set<String>) -> String

    @invariant EuropeanClosedForm
        -- A European call is valued by the Black-Scholes-Merton formula
        -- with the dividend treatment below.

    @invariant AmericanByLattice
        -- An American call is valued on a Leisen-Reimer binomial lattice
        -- (D-046), built on the spot reduced by the present value of the
        -- forecast cash dividends (OneDividendTreatment), with early
        -- exercise tested at every step; the number of steps is fixed by the
        -- recipe, never
        -- adaptive; evaluation is single-threaded and reproducible (02,
        -- DeterministicComputation).

    @invariant OneDividendTreatment
        -- Both exercise styles read the same dividend schedule (08: forecast
        -- cash amounts near term, a yield further out) and the same
        -- treatment, so that their values are comparable. The treatment is
        -- escrowed dividends (D-054): the spot is reduced by the present
        -- value of the cash dividends to the option's expiry before pricing,
        -- exactly for the closed form and as the lattice's starting spot for
        -- the American style; the treatment is named in the recipe and the
        -- result.

    @invariant VolatilityFromSurface
        -- The volatility is the surface's implied volatility at the option's
        -- strike and expiry (08), one value for the whole life of the option;
        -- no local or stochastic volatility in v1. The surface point used is
        -- part of the result.

    @invariant AmericanNotBelowEuropean
        -- For the same terms, the American value is never below the European
        -- value, and without dividends they are equal within the lattice
        -- tolerance (early exercise of a call on a non-dividend payer is
        -- never optimal). A violation is a deterministic rejection naming
        -- the figures.

    @invariant SeasonedOptionsAreOpenOnly
        -- An option at or after expiry at as_of is a deterministic rejection
        -- saying it expired (the position should have been closed by a
        -- succession, D-023); an exercised option is a succession into the
        -- delivered position.

    @invariant LifeCycleThroughSuccession
        -- Exercise, partial close-out (quantity reduction), expiry and
        -- amendment arrive as successions of the position (D-023); the
        -- valuation always uses the position version effective at as_of.
        -- Corporate actions (split, special dividend, merger, delisting): a
        -- feed proposes them, and each proposal becomes a succession of the
        -- affected option positions only after a person has reviewed and
        -- confirmed it (D-054); an unconfirmed proposal changes nothing and
        -- is listed until decided. The feed's source is an open question.

    @invariant ContractTerms
        -- The contract multiplier is part of the option security's terms and
        -- shared by every holder; a position holds only its quantity of
        -- contracts (D-054).

    @invariant ResultShape
        -- The valuation result contains (D-046): PV in local currency (the
        -- option currency) and in each requested reporting currency with the
        -- FX object used (D-033); intrinsic value and time value; the inputs
        -- actually used, so a reviewer can reproduce the figure: spot, strike,
        -- expiry as exact year fraction, discount factor, the volatility and
        -- its surface point, the dividend schedule and treatment, the
        -- exercise style and the lattice recipe hash and steps; quantity and
        -- contract multiplier.

    @invariant InformationalGreeks
        -- Delta, gamma and vega are reported for desks and are informational:
        -- regulatory sensitivities always come from the prescribed shocked
        -- graph nodes (D-032) and never from these figures.

    @invariant PlainVanillaOnly
        -- v1 (D-046): call options on one stock, in the stock's currency,
        -- physically settled, single exercise style per trade, fixed strike
        -- and expiry. Other terms are a deterministic rejection naming the
        -- feature.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor PricingWorker {
    identified_by: data/User
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface EquityOptionValuationComputation {
    facing worker: PricingWorker

    contracts:
        demands EquityOptionValuation
        demands graph/DeterministicComputation

    @guarantee GoldenCrossCheck
        -- Golden European and American valuations, with and without
        -- dividends, are reproduced independently (QuantLib, D-010) within a
        -- stated tolerance before a model version can enter a production
        -- release.

    @guarantee UpstreamContribution
        -- The Leisen-Reimer lattice, discrete-dividend handling and the
        -- Black-Scholes-Merton routines are generic and live in a separate
        -- package free of Veridian dependencies, offered to JuliaActuary
        -- first (D-031).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved (D-046): Black-Scholes-Merton for European, Leisen-Reimer lattice
-- for American; one volatility per option read from the surface; result
-- shape as above; informational Greeks.
-- Resolved (D-054): escrowed dividends for both styles; the default risk
-- charge of an option position takes the delta-adjusted notional of the
-- underlying (spec 11); multiplier on the security and quantity on the
-- position; corporate actions come from a feed and are applied only after a
-- person's review and confirmation.
open question "Source of the corporate actions feed under the rule of no paid data subscriptions: a free source, the clearing house notices, or manual entry that the feed later replaces?"
open question "Under the escrowed treatment an American call has no early-exercise premium from dividends: in the golden vectors (spot 100, strike 95, cash dividends of 1.0 at three and nine months) the escrowed American lattice gives 12.662285 and the European 12.662286, while a spot-dividend finite-difference cross-check gives 12.803288, 0.14 or about 1.1 % higher. Is that difference acceptable for the books, or should the American style use the spot-dividend treatment while the European keeps the closed form? The model validation (21) decides with these figures."
